window.docContents[197].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-trees":{"contents":"A graph is acyclic (a forest) if it contains no cycles. A tree is a connected\nforest.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity","header":"10.2. Trees","id":"/Connectivity/#section-trees"},"/Distance/#section-moore-and-cage-bounds":{"contents":"Fix a vertex r (for root). The distance layers around r are the sets of\nvertices at distance exactly 0, 1, 2, … from r: they are the levels of a\nbreadth-first search tree rooted at r, as in the figure of the Petersen graph in\nMoore Graphs. Comparing the sizes of consecutive layers gives two\nclassical bounds relating the number of vertices of a graph to its degrees, its\ndiameter and its girth.\n\n* The Moore bound: a graph with maximum degree at most d + 1 and diameter at most\n  k has at most 1 + (d + 1) * (1 + d + ⋯ + d ^ (k - 1)) vertices.* The cage bound: a graph with minimum degree at least d + 1 and girth at least\n  2 * k + 1 has at least 1 + (d + 1) * (1 + d + ⋯ + d ^ (k - 1)) vertices.\n\nNote that we write the degree bounds as d + 1 so that the formulas do not use\ntruncated subtraction in ℕ.\n\nProof sketch: write Lᵢ for the i-th layer around r, so L₀ = {r} and L₁ is\nthe neighbourhood of r.\n\n* Moore: each u ∈ Lᵢ with i ≥ 1 has a neighbour in Lᵢ₋₁, so it has at most d\n  neighbours in Lᵢ₊₁. Hence #Lᵢ₊₁ ≤ d * #Lᵢ, and if the diameter is at most k\n  then the layers L₀, …, Lₖ cover V.* Cage: if the girth is at least 2 * k + 1 then the graph looks like a tree near\n  r. For 1 ≤ i < k, all the neighbours of u ∈ Lᵢ but one lie in Lᵢ₊₁, and\n  two vertices of Lᵢ have no common neighbour there, since otherwise there would\n  be a cycle of length at most 2 * i + 2 ≤ 2 * k. Hence #Lᵢ₊₁ ≥ d * #Lᵢ, and the\n  disjoint layers L₀, …, Lₖ contain the claimed number of vertices.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance","header":"11.4. Moore and Cage Bounds","id":"/Distance/#section-moore-and-cage-bounds"},"/Subgraphs/#section-deleting-vertices-and-edges":{"contents":"If H : G.Subgraph then we can form a new subgraph by deleting any set of vertices\ns : Set V from H (while also obviously removing any edges incident to those\nvertices).\n\nVertex deletion is defined by taking the subgraph of H induced by H.verts \\ s.\n\nexample (s : Set V) {H : G.Subgraph} :\n  H.deleteVerts s = H.induce (H.verts \\ s) := rfl\n\n\nA pair of vertices v w : V are adjacent in the subgraph obtained by deleting a\nset of vertices s : Set V from a subgraph H : G.Subgraph if and only if they\nare not in s and are adjacent in H. (Mathlib's Subgraph.deleteVerts_adj states\nthis with the redundant conditions v ∈ H.verts and w ∈ H.verts included.)\n\nexample {v w : V} (s : Set V) {H : G.Subgraph} :\n    (H.deleteVerts s).Adj v w ↔ v ∉ s ∧ w ∉ s ∧ H.Adj v w := by\n  grind [Subgraph.induce_adj, H.edge_vert, Subgraph.Adj.symm]\n\n\nWe can also delete a set of edges s : Set (Sym2 V) from a subgraph (while leaving\nthe vertex set unchanged).\n\n#source Subgraph.deleteEdges -- G'.deleteEdges s : G.Subgraph\n\n\nexample {v w : V} (s : Set (Sym2 V)) {H : G.Subgraph} :\n    (H.deleteEdges s).Adj v w ↔ H.Adj v w ∧ s(v, w) ∉ s := by rfl\n\n\n#check Subgraph.deleteEdges_verts -- (G'.deleteEdges s).verts = G'.verts\n\n\nLeft: a graph G. Centre: deleting the vertices v_1 and v_3, which also removes their edges. Right: deleting the edges v_1 v_4 and v_2 v_5. Removed vertices and edges are dashed\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Constructing Subgraphs","header":"9.2.3. Deleting Vertices and Edges","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-deleting-vertices-and-edges"}});